b/ref 17592186045417] [17592186156688 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186156688 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "dec569bc-4861-5f9b-9002-2902f4b4c77f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186156689 #edb/ref 1080 "images/95/95f55293ecb8a57ce34c50dfb11b5090923137273e4885e99bd54cae7b125e4f.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186156689 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186156689 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e6afc5cb-aafa-572c-bf4f-2e18147ce5e1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186156690 #edb/ref 1080 "images/ac/acafb0f5cec6fc2ad238fa9a1a53fee47fe8f0e37ded3c7733ec838a6c1d66e2.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186156690 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186156690 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "075982d7-3367-5e34-a8c4-bb90d5e49ddb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186156691 #edb/ref 1080 "images/af/af8a5de34c0cb4d24879c6dc211e8f610eab0a5602985ac82c43cd4db3c2d49e.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186156691 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186156691 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0a533d56-e848-502f-ba53-cd04f2196f4b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186156692 #edb/ref 1080 "images/72/721db846a92d0ede38f6234154c54f55fe1bb3c5cae852e900864b1da1de2a5a.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186156692 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186156692 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4a3df525-d1dd-5895-9bfa-0e38c6d44d01" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157167 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7820" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157167 #edb/ref 1071 "Plot the graph of $x= \\dfrac{1}{2}y - \\sqrt y\\,$ by completing the following table:\n\n| **$y$** | $0$ | $1$ | $4$ | $16$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x$ |  |  |  |  |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157167 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dfd30d6a-23f2-5304-8cb1-cc3a0f31463e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157167 #edb/ref 1074 "To graph this curve, we can start by calculating the value of $x$ for various values of $y$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}y = 0 & ⟶x = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 0 - \\sqrt{0} = 0 \\\\ y = 1 & ⟶x = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 1 - \\sqrt{1} =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ y = 4 & ⟶x = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 4 - \\sqrt{4} = 0 \\\\ y = 16 & ⟶x = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 16 - \\sqrt{16} = 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can summarize these results in the following table:\n\n| **$y$** | $0$ | $1$ | $4$ | $16$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-\\dfrac12$ | $0$ | $4$ |\n\nSo, we have the following points:\n\n$$\n(0,0), \\quad \\left(-\\dfrac 12,1\\right), \\quad (0,4), \\quad (4,16)\n$$\n\nIf we plot these points and connect them with a smooth curve, we get the following graph:\n\n![](images/70/709bfcd1e69a0e4fd88d95fb9d8592de93921fad230b5f892059525871dc50fb.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157168 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7821" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157168 #edb/ref 1071 "The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below. Sketch the graph of $x=f(y)$.\n\n![](images/76/76268e0ba23b56af4c7215a4a44f459e6a3ea42454f53df1d39894df16513865.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157168 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1a638e6a-e5ab-5a60-81f5-2847e917b456" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157168 #edb/ref 1074 "To plot the graph of $x=f(y)$, we take the graph of $y=f(x)$ and reflect it over the line $y=x$, as follows:\n\n![](images/2e/2e11bec1e27fd59a1b4ea4e44070591acf5cd8fefebe6d5e1a46ef0be3f5129f.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157169 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7822" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157169 #edb/ref 1071 "Plot the graph of $x=-2y^2 - 3y$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157169 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e6282f0a-7891-5261-968e-63aa42421d00" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157169 #edb/ref 1074 "To plot $x=-2y^2-3y$, we will first plot the function $y=-2x^2-3x$ and then reflect it over the line $y=x$.\n\nThe curve $y=-2x^2-3x$ is a parabola. It has the following factored form:\n\n$$\ny = -x(2x+3)\n$$\n\nFrom the factored form above, we see that we have a negative parabola with roots at $x=0$ and\n\n$$\nx=-\\dfrac32\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following graph:\n\n![](images/c2/c2a1492655ff33a49bd21e911b13b99d67191a4cd39683f34fca4be295dda41a.png)\n\nNow, to get the curve $x=-2y^2-3y$, we just have to reflect the curve $y=-2x^2-3x$ over the line $y=x$, as follows:\n\n![](images/7f/7fbfb3fd5bbacbf1c974bf654e993a7203934db5d679bfaa89ddc0c54e39a7df.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157352 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157352 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050790 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157352 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157955 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157352 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157960 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157352 #edb/ref 1039 "Plotting X as a Function of Y Using a Table of Values" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157352 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186157167 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157352 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "109b0bfb-10fe-5b31-adb2-be746ef71936" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157353 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048686 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157353 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157956 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157353 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157962 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157353 #edb/ref 1039 "Plotting X as a Function of Y Given the Graph of the Corresponding Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157353 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186157168 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157353 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "51ae30bd-569c-52dd-96a0-4244578db5bf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157354 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049244 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157354 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157957 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157354 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157961 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157354 #edb/ref 1039 "Plotting a Quadratic Function Where X Is a Function of Y" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157354 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186157169 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157354 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "1f2981f6-bfbf-5c1e-a4a2-2814dc24804a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157526 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158225 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157526 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158226 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157526 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158227 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157526 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158228 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157526 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158229 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157526 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157526 #edb/ref 1045 "Plotting X as a Function of Y" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157526 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186158225 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157526 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049178 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157526 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "08c34207-4158-5cd6-b7c7-eda8eb60b32c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157961 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186156687 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157961 #edb/ref 1069 "q-88507" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157961 #edb/ref 1071 "Plot the graph of $x =-y^{2} - 3y$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157961 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "803745d4-7e67-5cee-b697-645c72bb764f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158225 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158226 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158225 #edb/ref 1061 29069 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158225 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ad692cd2-b2aa-553b-af11-d2a2e96f2938" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158225 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186158528 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158226 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158229 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158226 #edb/ref 1061 29070 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158226 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "97eff80c-79f1-5938-b354-b7a812934467" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158226 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157352 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158227 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158228 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158227 #edb/ref 1061 29071 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158227 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8a56a63c-886a-5511-8e90-a5a786cbac97" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158227 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157353 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158228 #edb/ref 1061 29072 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158228 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4dfeda37-1f1e-58ac-897c-5bb43d56241a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158228 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157354 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158229 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158227 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158229 #edb/ref 1061 29075 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158229 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fe2be374-df26-589e-a401-2e869139d14d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158229 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186158529 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158528 #edb/ref 1064 "We're used to plotting curves where $y$ is a function of $x$. However, we can also plot curves where $x$ is a function of $y$.\n\nFor example, consider the curve $x = y^2$. To graph this curve, we can start by calculating the values of $x$ for some values of $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}y =-3 & ⟶x = (-3)^{2} = 9 \\\\ y =-2 & ⟶x = (-2)^{2} = 4 \\\\ y =-1 & ⟶x = (-1)^{2} = 1 \\\\ y = 0 & ⟶x = 0^{2} = 0 \\\\ y = 1 & ⟶x = 1^{2} = 1 \\\\ y = 2 & ⟶x = 2^{2} = 4 \\\\ y = 3 & ⟶x = 3^{2} = 9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can summarize these results in the following table:\n\n| **$y$** | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x$ | $9$ | $4$ | $1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $4$ | $9$ |\n\nSo, we have the following points:\n\n$$\n(9,-3), \\quad (4,-2), \\quad (1,-1), \\quad (0,0), \\quad (1,1), \\quad (4,2), \\quad (9,3)\n$$\n\nIf we plot these points and connect them with a smooth curve, we get the following graph:\n\n![](images/ae/aec45789560229c23f0c86069bad46593a7d00a637f6f8da33d36634ae504e52.png)\n\nWe say that the equation of the curve is defined **implicitly**, because instead of the usual $y = f(x)$, we have $x = f(y)$.\n\n**Watch out!** Here, we are viewing $x=y^2$ not as a function but rather as an equation. A point belongs to the curve if and only if its coordinates satisfy the equation. The curve does not represent a function since it fails the vertical line test." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158528 #edb/ref 1065 4707 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158528 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "3217d90b-5727-5445-a812-db001667675c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158528 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158529 #edb/ref 1064 "Another way to plot $x = f(y)$ is to first plot the corresponding function $y = f(x)$ and then reflect it over the line $y=x$.\n\nTo illustrate, let's use this technique to plot the curve $x=y^2$. To get the corresponding function, we swap $x$ and $y$ and get $y=x^2$. This curve is familiar and much easier to graph:\n\n![](images/af/afca2357f9d26876cedc7fb1d059c95c8906d9144f2fdb17dd2f61eec878a196.png)\n\nNow, to get the curve $x=y^2$, we just have to reflect the curve $y=x^2$ over the line $y=x$, as follows:\n\n![](images/5b/5b91959bd83265ed0b606072b26029bd995f51a8128c14367af9013688db148c.png)\n\nYou may notice that this process is very similar to plotting the inverse of a function. In fact, if $y=f(x)$ is a one-to-one function, then the corresponding function $x = f(y)$ will always give the inverse function." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158529 #edb/ref 1065 4708 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158529 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ef9c9a8e-f3c3-57cf-936a-e4586282e650" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158529 #edb/ref 1067 "Plotting X as a Function of Y Using a Reflection" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
